# What may enter the build context — an allow-list, not a deny-list.
#
# A deny-list is one forgotten line away from shipping the answer key. The team
# holds both hackathon packages: the participant bundle, which the organisers
# confirmed may be published, and the organisers' own package, which was
# mis-sent and whose README says not to hand it to participants. The latter is
# `data/_grader/` — ground_truth.json plus the dataset generator — and a
# published container image is a handing-over as surely as a public repository
# is (CLAUDE.md rule 1, docs/SCORING.md).
#
# So: exclude everything, then name back the two directories the API actually
# needs. Anything added to the repository later is excluded by default, which
# is the right default when the cost of one mistake is the whole dataset.

*

!backend/
!demo_data/

# --- re-excluded from inside the allow-list --------------------------------
# `!backend/` takes the whole tree, including things the running API never
# touches.

# 525 tests, their fixtures and conftest. pytest and httpx are not installed in
# the image (backend/requirements-api.txt), so these could not run there even
# if something tried.
backend/tests/

# The adversarial harness and the reader smoke test. Both are developer tools
# that read the git-ignored data/ to do anything at all, so in a container that
# has no data/ they are dead weight with a misleading name.
backend/tools/

# Host bytecode is cp310-win_amd64 and the image is cp310-linux: stale, wrong
# and silently shadowing the real source if the timestamps line up.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]

# --- belt and braces -------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below is already excluded by `*` above. It is repeated last, and
# deliberately last, because .dockerignore resolves by last match: if someone
# later adds a `!data/...` line to get one file in, these lines still win and
# the answer key still does not ship. Losing a needed file is a build error you
# see immediately; leaking this one is not something you can take back.

.git/
.env
.env.local
data/
runs/
.cache/
.venv/
*.zip
ground_truth.json
submission*.json

# Not secret, only useless in the image, and named here so the exclusion reads
# as intentional rather than accidental: the Next.js dashboard deploys to
# Vercel from the same repository (web/vercel.json), and node_modules/ or
# .next/ in this context would add hundreds of megabytes to every upload.
web/
